<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Future Careers – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Future Careers</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> To Become a Doctor, First I Will Need To...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Explain a recent learning experience. Interactive: Engage in questions and answers about future goals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain a past learning experience relevant to a future career by answering questions and sharing reflections (e.g., "What did you learn during your presentation?" "I learned to speak clearly and organize my ideas because I want to be a teacher."). Can ask and answer questions about personal career goals (e.g., "What are your plans for the future?" "I want to be a flight attendant."). Can respond to questions about personal goals related to specific professions (e.g., "What do you need to do to become a flight attendant?" "I need to study languages and have good communication skills.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of different professionals (doctor, lawyer, teacher) and asks students: "What do you see?" "Which of these careers do you know?" "What do you want to be in the future?" Students quickly share their initial ideas with a partner. This activates prior vocabulary from previous lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models expressing future goals and asking about them. "I want to be a teacher because I like helping others." (Teacher points to a teacher picture). "What do you want to be, [Student Name]?" The teacher clearly models the pronunciation of "want to be" and "will need to."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of future intentions. "When I say 'I want to be a doctor', am I talking about yesterday or the future?" (Expected answer: The future.) "Am I telling you what I want, or what someone else wants?" (Expected answer: What I want.)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Future Careers."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short dialogue (on a projector or handout) between two teenagers, Maria and Carlos, discussing their future career plans. The dialogue incorporates Wh-questions for career goals, future tense (e.g., "I will need to study"), modals for intention and suggestions (e.g., "I would like to get an internship"), and "want to + infinitive." It also includes target vocabulary (e.g., doctor, lawyer, career, goals, plan, prepare).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Dialogue Snippet:</i><br>Maria: "Hi, Carlos! What do you want to be in the future?"<br>Carlos: "I want to be a doctor. It's a challenging but important career.<br>Maria: "Wow! That’s a big goal. What will you need to do to achieve that?"<br>Carlos: "First, I will need to finish high school successfully, then study hard for university. I would like to get an internship in a hospital one day."<br>Maria: "That sounds like a great plan! I want to be a lawyer. I will need to be very professional."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interacting orally to express future plans and justify intentions related to career goals.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (oral):</b> "Who are the people talking?" "What are they talking about?" "What careers do they mention?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (oral, whole class):</b> "Maria wants to be a doctor." "Carlos will need to study hard for university."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using Wh-questions for career goals, future tense for plans ("I will need to..."), modals for intention and suggestions ("I would like to..."), and "want to + infinitive verb." Target vocabulary: doctor, lawyer, teacher, career, goals, internship, qualifications, plan, practice, train, achieve, succeed, prepare, professional, skilled, important. Pronunciation of modals: should, would, will (/ʊ/ and /ɪ/). Phonemic awareness: Minimal pairs (/ʃ/ in "ship" versus /tʃ/ in "chip," and /dʒ/ in "joke" versus /tʃ/ in "choke").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Gap-fill & Read Aloud):</b> Students receive a short dialogue similar to the presentation, but with blanks for target grammar (e.g., "I ____ to be a doctor." "I ____ need to study hard."). Students fill in the blanks individually, then work in pairs to read the completed dialogue aloud, focusing on accurate pronunciation and intonation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Substitution Drill & Pronunciation Focus):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sentence Builder:</b> Teacher provides a sentence frame: "I want to be a _______ because I like _______." Students take turns substituting vocabulary words (e.g., lawyer, teacher, doctor) and verbs (e.g., helping, solving problems, caring).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Modal & Minimal Pair Drill:</b> The teacher leads a choral and individual repetition drill focusing on the pronunciation of modals: "should" (/ʃʊd/), "would" (/wʊd/), "will" (/wɪl/). Students practice saying sentences like "I would like to learn," "I will prepare," "You should train." Additionally, the teacher introduces and drills minimal pairs such as "ship" /ʃɪp/ vs "chip" /tʃɪp/, "share" /ʃɛr/ vs "chair" /tʃɛr/, and "joke" /dʒoʊk/ vs "choke" /tʃoʊk/, ensuring students can differentiate and produce the sounds clearly in isolation and in simple phrases (e.g., "a professional challenge" /tʃ/ vs "a professional suggestion" /dʒ/).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Q&A Exchange):</b> Students work in pairs. Student A asks "What do you want to be in the future?" Student B responds using "I want to be a [career] because..." and "I will need to [action]..." Then, Student B asks Student A, "What are your plans to achieve that goal?" Student A responds using "I plan to..." and "I would like to..." They use the practiced grammar and vocabulary, providing suggestions for improvement if needed (Sociolinguistic competence).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Future Careers."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-playing a "Career Day Interview." Students will work in pairs to simulate an interview between a Career Counselor and a Student.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> This task directly connects to "Future Careers" and specifically "To Become a Doctor, First I Will Need To..." as students must articulate their chosen career path and the steps required.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Role Assignment:</b> Students divide into pairs. One student acts as the "Career Counselor" (Student A), and the other acts as the "Student" planning their future (Student B).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Interview Simulation:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A (Counselor) asks questions using Wh-questions for career goals: "What do you want to be in the future?" "Why do you want to become a [career]?" "What did you learn during your [e.g., last science project/presentation] that is important for this career?" "What will you need to do to achieve your goals?" "What qualifications would you like to get?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student B (Student) responds using "I want to be...", "I will need to...", "I plan to...", and "I would like to...", explaining their intentions and a past learning experience relevant to their chosen career. E.g., "I want to be a doctor. I learned teamwork in my science class, which is important for doctors. I will need to study biology and chemistry. I would like to successfully complete an internship."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Role Swap:</b> After 2-3 minutes, students swap roles and repeat the interview.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>4. Fluency Focus:</b> The teacher encourages fluent interaction, natural pauses, and the use of the target vocabulary and grammar structures discussed throughout the lesson. The emphasis is on clear communication and completing the communicative task.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on oral production. Peer assessment checklist where students evaluate their partner’s speaking performance during the role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "Career Day Interview" role-play for the teacher or for another pair, demonstrating their ability to ask and answer questions about future goals and explain relevant learning experiences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary and modals (e.g., /ʊ/ in 'would', 'should').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate and consistent use of "want to + infinitive," future tense ("will need to"), and modals ("would like to").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to ask and answer Wh-questions about personal career goals effectively.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence in explaining a past learning experience and future plans.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active participation and collaborative interaction during the interview.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I learned about my future career goals today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy/difficult to (circle one) __________ because __________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about discussing your future career plans in English today, and how did you try to overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did practicing with a partner help you express your ideas more clearly or confidently?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in terms of student engagement and achievement of the speaking objective? (e.g., "Students actively used 'I want to be' and 'I will need to' when discussing their goals."). What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson or future speaking activities? (e.g., "Some students struggled with the correct pronunciation of /ʊ/ in 'would' and 'should'; I will incorporate more targeted drills next time." or "Need to provide more sentence starters for explaining past learning experiences.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Today we practiced speaking about our future career plans. Tomorrow, we will take these ideas and use them to write a short paragraph, describing our chosen career and the steps we plan to take, focusing on clear sentence structure and vocabulary."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>